0. Introduction

We  expect a  consistent coding  style  across all  of Matanza's  source
files: the one described in this file.

The ideas in  this file are subject  to discusion by the  members of the
Matanza Core.

1. Source layout

GNU's Coding Standards must be followed as closely as possible.

The following indentation is right:

void
function_set ( int a )
{
  /* Here goes a silly comment that explains something useful and is broken
   * into lines like this. */

  if (!a)
    {
      printf("Bad.\n");
      exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
  else
    printf("Hey.\n");
}

Note that for multiple-words functions (most  of them), we write them as
mat_foo_bar_set,  rather than  MatFooBarSet.   Also note  that we  don't
leave spaces after function calls but do leave spaces after control-flow
keywords (such as if or for).

Preprocessor defines must always be defined entirely in uppercase.

Try to  enter numerical constants directly  into your program as  few as
possible.  Instead,  define them  as preprocessor  macros and  given the
appropriate names.  Even if you don't  expect their values to ever vary,
this should make  the code much more readable as  it becomes now obvious
why the given numbers were used.

To comment out code  you must use #if 0 / #endif  around it.  This makes
it easy  to add  it or remove  it, without having  to care  about nested
comments.

