2. Object Oriented Programming

Matanza's source code must be  object oriented: the important structures
or modules must be declared in their own files.

For each  important structure, you must  declare one header file  with a
typedef for the structure.  The actual definition for the structure must
never go in the header file: all access to the function's fields must be
provided through  functions defined  in the  associated code  file.  The
header file must only include the typedef, #defines for constants needed
and the prototypes for the object's functions.

This is to  provide real encapsulation of data.  We  can always begin to
inline critic functions if necessary.

The C  source file can only  declare public symbols for  those listed in
the header file.  All other symbols must be declared static.  The public
functions must  begin with  the prefix mat_object_  where object  is the
name of your object.

Your object must be named as MatObject.   Both your type as well as your
structure's name must have the same name.  Never typedef your objects as
pointers, we want to be able to tell pointers from structures easily.

The functions ending in _get must never  modify the object in any way so
we  can use  them  in  our assertions  and  don't  change our  program's
behaviour when we build it without assertions.

If  you provide  constructors  and destructors,  they  should be  called
mat_object_new  and mat_object_free.   If you  want to  provide multiple
constructors  (destructors),  they  must  be  called  mat_object_new_XXX
(mat_object_free_XXX).  It is recommended that you call the one that you
expect people to use the most mat_object_new (mat_object_free).

3. Assertions

Use them as much as you can.

It doesn't matter  if you think it  will make the code slow:  we will be
able to remove them in our stable public releases.

For the assertions use the ASSERT macro.

Also, it is okay  to fill your code with calls to  the VERBOSE macro for
important sections.  However, try not to  make way too many calls to it:
it could turn  out to be extremely noisy.  Basically,  leave the VERBOSE
calls in  sections that you think  might give trouble in  the future but
remove it from well-tested stable sections.

The only exception  to the assert-everything rule is  complex deep loops
where the assertions would make the code significantly slow.

In  your  assertions  you  are  only  allowed  to  call  Matanza-defined
functions that end  in _get and standard functions  that obviously don't
have side effects.

However, do fill with assertions all  the complex code.  It is suggested
that you add assertions for both pre and post conditions.

Having  Matanza full  of  assertions  has helped  us  find  bugs a  lot.
Debugging Matanza is as funny as building it with assertions, running it
under a  debugger with a breakpoint  set on the exit  function (to catch
the program's state  should an assertion fail) and  playing.  Sure beats
most debugging situations.

So don't forget this: Use assertions as much as you can.

4. Use of CVS

The rule for  CVS is commit whenever you make  even the smallest change.
Try never to commit multiple changes at the same time.

Actually, it is okay  to submit a lot of changes  in a batch.  Otherwise
it would be way too complicated to  work in Matanza offline.  But if you
can, try to follow the above rule.

If you  have doubts  about whether  your changes  are ok,  consult other
members of the core for suggestions before actually submitting them.

Never change just the indentation of a line.  Even if the indentation is
obviously wrong, you  don't want to change that line  if everything else
is fine with it.  Doing this would  defeat the purpose of using a system
to control versions.

5. Commenting Code

There are two  things that you must never leave  uncommented: the fields
in your structure and the prototypes in your C header file.

It is considered a serious bug when the above rule is violated.

Other than  that, you shouldn't really  comment your code: it  should be
written in a way that it is obvious.

The problem  with overcommented code  is that  when one needs  to change
something, one  needs to read  the whole  comments and update  them.  In
this situation,  it is  preferable to throw  the paragraphs  of comments
away.

When you comment your code say what it does but not how it does it.  How
it does it is very silly as your  code already shows that in a much more
precise way.

On the  other hand, if you  use complex theorems to  optimize things, it
would be nice  if you could explain  where you got your  lovely ideas so
other programmers can  understand all the work you did  in order to know
that writing the code in your non-obvious way would still work.

6. Debugging

This section does not deeply affect  the style of Matanza's source code,
but gives you advice on how to debug it.

The prefered  way of debugging  is using assertions  everywhere (through
the ASSERT macro).   When that is not enough, you  are encouraged to use
the  VERBOSE macro  to have  Matanza print  information about  its state
(you'll need  to define MATANZA_VERBOSE  to 1 when building  and execute
matanza with the --verbose option).

When none  of this  works, it  is okay  to use  GDB or  another system's
debugger (if you are unlucky to be outside of Unix).

To check for  memory leaks, we advice you to  use the wonderful CCMalloc
package.  You will want to pass Matanza the undocumented (at least not
appearing in the --help message) --free-at-exit parameter.

You might also  want to build Matanza with Bruce  Perens' Electric Fence
to have it check for memory corruption.
